---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="college-degrees"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>The professional courses for commerce students that matter are CA, CS and CMA in India, and ACCA, CFA, FRM and the US CPA if you want a global route.</strong>
    Each one is a different bet on years, money and what you are legally allowed to do. For CA, CS and the US CPA the credential is the product, because
    only the holder can do the work. For the rest it is a strong signal that works best next to proof of work. Picking the right one is a
    decision that unlocks high income opportunities and a high-income skill portfolio, and a wrong pick costs years.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The answer:</strong> CA, CS and CMA are the Indian routes and can start after Class 10 or 12. ACCA, CFA, FRM and the US CPA are global routes that usually sit on top of a degree and need work experience.</li>
      <li><strong>The decision filter:</strong> Ask whether the credential is legally required for the work (CA audit, US CPA licence) or only a signal. Then set the full cost and time against your whole education budget, about 10% as a starting limit, and avoid a loan unless it is legally required.</li>
      <li><strong>The cost trap:</strong> ICAI registration fees add to Rs 49,000. The real bill is repeat attempts, coaching and low stipends. In May 2026 only 8.47% passed CA Intermediate in both groups.</li>
      <li><strong>The next step:</strong> Pick one lane, do one real task from it, and read the body's own rules page before you pay anyone.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

